  "articleBody": "When planning accessible kitchen outlet placement, it's easy to make a few common blunders that can really impact usability. One frequent mistake is placing outlets too high or too far back, making them a struggle to reach, especially from a seated position. A simple fix is to always consider the reach range for someone using a wheelchair. Another common error is overlooking the clear floor space needed in front of the outlet; without it, access is severely limited. Always ensure there's enough room for a wheelchair to maneuver directly in front of where someone needs to plug something in. People often assume that a tidy, aesthetically pleasing location is automatically accessible, but that's not always the case – function has to come before pure form here. Finally, don't forget about counter depth, islands, peninsulas, or other obstructions that might block access to plugs. It's crucial to account for these elements in your design. If you're undertaking a kitchen remodel, particularly one focused on accessibility, a professional can help you navigate these pitfalls and ensure your kitchen is truly functional for everyone.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
}